  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 308 PM EDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nGOES satellite imagery and RAP analysis show a deep, well defined \nlow pressure system crossing into northern Ontario this afternoon, \nwith a trailing surface cold front extending south into Minnesota \nand very light precipitation associated with the warm front \ndeparting the eastern UP. This places Upper Michigan squarely within \nthe warm sector of this system, with temperatures increasing into \nthe low 70s across much of the area this afternoon. Breezy southerly \nwinds are producing gale force gusts over eastern Lake Superior and \nnorthern Lake Michigan as unstable boundary layer conditions help \nwinds from a 40 kt low level jet mix down to the surface.\n\nWinds shift west-northwesterly tonight as the cold front sweeps \nacross the UP. This will re-establish a cooler northwest flow, cold \nair advection regime as upper level troughing sits over the James \nBay region through Thursday. Still expect temperatures to make into \nthe 60s across most of the area on Wednesday given relatively modest \ncold advection, eventually settling back into the 50s on Thursday. \nScattered, light lake effect rain showers will redevelop tonight and \nlinger through Thursday, mainly across the east as 850 mb temps cool \nback below 0 C and lake surface temperatures remain in the 13-15 C \nrange over the eastern half of Lake Superior. Expect very little \nrainfall out of this activity with amounts generally limited to a \nfew hundredths of an inch over the next few days. The patter will \nalso support breezy west-northwest winds through the end of the work \nweek, frequently gusting to 25-30 mph over the Keweenaw and Lake \nSuperior shoreline and 15-20 mph over the rest of the UP. Dewpoints \nshould mix out nicely each afternoon, introducing some limited fire \nweather concerns over the next few days as RH values drop into the \n30s in interior locations each day.\n\nWarmer weather returns this weekend as the upper level pattern is \namplified by a deep trough digging over the western CONUS, placing \nthe Upper Midwest in deep southwest flow and pushing 850 mb temps to \n18-20 C over the UP. This should correlate to widespread temps in \nthe 70s both Saturday and Sunday, with some locations in the west \nand along the WI border currently showing a greater than 50% chance \nto reach 80 degrees per the NBM. Also expect the forecast to stay \ndry through the weekend as guidance depicts one system with tropical \norigins tracking north from the Gulf Coast but staying well to our \nsoutheast, while precipitation associated with another low moving \nout of the northern Rockies stays to the north over Ontario.",
